#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 15.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 86.06pt, "1. Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 118.46pt, "Hydrogen and oxygen production via water electrolysis (i.e., water splitting) requires catalysts that are both ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 137.42pt, "active and stable, as well as electrically conductive under operating conditions. Precious metal-based ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 156.38pt, "catalysts, such as platinum and iridium dioxide, are effective for catalyzing water splitting in acidic media; ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 175.46pt, "however, they are expensive and scarce")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(253.25pt, 171.5pt, "1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(256.73pt, 175.46pt, ". In contrast, the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) in alkaline ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 194.42pt, "environments is typically performed using non-noble transition-metal oxide (TMO) catalysts")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(488.26pt, 190.46pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(491.74pt, 194.42pt, ". However, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 213.38pt, "these generally exhibit lower activity compared to their noble-metal counterparts in acidic media. As a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 232.34pt, "result, significant efforts have been directed toward developing inexpensive, efficient, earth-abundant, and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 251.33pt, "environmentally benign electrocatalysts for these reactions")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(333.43pt, 247.37pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(336.91pt, 251.33pt, ". Nickel oxide (NiO) is a promising electrode ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 270.29pt, "material for the alkaline OER due to its low cost, natural abundance, and favorable redox properties")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(512.14pt, 266.33pt, "4")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(515.62pt, 270.29pt, ". As a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 289.25pt, "p-type semiconductor with a wide energy gap (>3.0 eV)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(326.95pt, 285.29pt, "5")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(330.43pt, 289.25pt, ", NiO features partially filled Ni 3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(486.94pt, 289.25pt, "d")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(492.46pt, 289.25pt, " states that ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 308.21pt, "hybridize with O 2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(152.66pt, 308.21pt, "p")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(158.18pt, 308.21pt, " orbitals, providing a basis for redox activity. When immersed in an alkaline electrolyte, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 327.17pt, "its surface readily converts to nickel hydroxide (Ni(OH)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(316.37pt, 328.13pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(319.87pt, 327.17pt, "), an n-type semiconductor with a wide energy gap ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 346.25pt, "(>3eV)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(102.74pt, 342.29pt, "6,7")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(111.5pt, 346.25pt, ", which, under anodic potentials, further transforms into the OER-active nickel oxyhydroxide ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 365.21pt, "(NiOOH, p-type semiconductor with a reduced energy gap)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(332.95pt, 361.25pt, "6,8")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(341.71pt, 365.21pt, ". ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 392.09pt, "Ni hydroxide/oxyhydroxide films are generally accessible via a redox reaction that involves electrochemical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 411.17pt, "activation of NiO films")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(172.1pt, 407.21pt, "9,10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(184.34pt, 411.17pt, " –which are typically prepared by dry, vacuum-based processes such as sputtering ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 430.15pt, "and pulsed laser deposition (PLD)– in an aqueous electrolyte solution. Nevertheless, only a few reports ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 449.11pt, "describe the direct preparation of NiOOH and Ni(OH)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(305.69pt, 450.07pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(309.17pt, 449.11pt, " films using dry methods")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(416.35pt, 445.15pt, "11,12")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(432.07pt, 449.11pt, ". Separately, several wet-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 468.07pt, "chemical routes–such as sol-gel, dip-coating, and cathodic deposition–can also yield Ni hydroxides and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 487.03pt, "oxyhydroxides")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(136.34pt, 483.07pt, "13–15")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(153.74pt, 487.03pt, ". Nonetheless, these reports often lack correlative spectroscopic evidence or systematic ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 505.99pt, "comparisons to confirm phase formation as-deposited. This absence of data leaves uncertainty as to whether ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 524.95pt, "hydroxide or oxyhydroxide phases, and the associated oxygen-hole states, can be stabilized before ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 543.91pt, "electrochemical treatments. Besides, even if dry or wet processes successfully form the NiOOH phase, its ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 562.87pt, "instability in air presents a significant challenge for its characterization; even brief exposure to the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 581.98pt, "atmosphere during transfer can degrade NiOOH; thus, by the time the sample is returned to vacuum to be ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 600.94pt, "analyzed spectroscopically, the surface may no longer reflect its initial state, as we have shown in recent ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 619.9pt, "studies")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(101.54pt, 615.94pt, "16,17")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(117.26pt, 619.9pt, ". Therefore, unambiguous identification of the active phase requires vacuum-integrated ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 638.86pt, "approaches such as X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), ensuring that the deposited phase can be ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 657.82pt, "analyzed without ambient degradation. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 684.82pt, "A further challenge lies in the electrochemical conditioning typically required to activate NiO. Converting ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 703.776pt, "NiO into Ni(OH)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(151.58pt, 704.736pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(155.06pt, 703.776pt, " often demands tens to hundreds of cyclic voltammetry (CV) cycles before stable ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 722.736pt, "properties are achieved")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(177.62pt, 718.776pt, "16,18")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
